WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.928
Can large language models combine skills they
already know?

00:00:05.008 --> 00:00:13.792
This COLM 2024 paper tests whether models solve an
unseen composite task by fusing two simple tasks seen separately.

00:00:13.872 --> 00:00:17.460
Across Llama and GPT, a sharp dichotomy emerges.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.725
Suppose a model learned two simple tasks in-context,

00:00:21.805 --> 00:00:25.308
capitalizing certain words and swapping others.

00:00:25.388 --> 00:00:29.502
Given an input needing both, can it combine the skills?

00:00:29.582 --> 00:00:34.610
For humans this is trivial, yet even GPT-4 and
Claude 3 often fail.

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:39.042
Models are increasingly asked to chain skills for
real reasoning,

00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:42.284
yet we lack a clear account of when chaining works.

00:00:42.364 --> 00:00:45.081
Prior studies are narrow with little theory,

00:00:45.161 --> 00:00:48.450
and the failure reproduces easily on frontier models.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:55.795
Three contributions: a test suite of linguistic and
logical composite tasks with simple-task examples only;

00:00:55.875 --> 00:01:00.206
evaluation across Llama and GPT scales revealing a
clear dichotomy;

00:01:00.286 --> 00:01:04.880
and a linear self-attention theory explaining when
composition emerges.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.632
Two halves.

00:01:08.712 --> 00:01:12.083
Empirically,

00:01:12.163 --> 00:01:15.535
each composite task is tested in four settings with
ten in-context examples: each simple task alone,

00:01:15.615 --> 00:01:18.987
a composite test with simple-task demos, and an
all-composite gold standard.

00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:22.438
Theoretically,

00:01:22.518 --> 00:01:25.890
a linear self-attention model shows composition
succeeds under confined support.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.975
The suite pairs simple building blocks.

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:32.546
Linguistic tasks include capitalization, swapping,

00:01:32.626 --> 00:01:37.189
and translations like phrase recombination and
passive-to-active.

00:01:37.269 --> 00:01:41.045
Logical tasks combine arithmetic with word operations.

00:01:41.125 --> 00:01:46.116
Tasks split into separable composites, acting on
different input parts,

00:01:46.196 --> 00:01:50.330
and compose-by-step composites requiring chained
reasoning.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.474
A sharp split.

00:01:54.554 --> 00:01:58.318
On separable composite tasks, models compose well
and improve with scale,

00:01:58.398 --> 00:02:02.162
approaching the gold standard.

00:02:02.242 --> 00:02:06.006
On compose-by-step tasks they collapse: Llama
solves each simple task near ninety percent,

00:02:06.086 --> 00:02:09.850
but the composite drops below twenty percent, and
scaling doesn't help.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.785
The scale sweep is the key ablation.

00:02:12.865 --> 00:02:16.079
On separable tasks accuracy rises with scale;

00:02:16.159 --> 00:02:19.813
on compose-by-step tasks it stays flat or degrades.

00:02:19.893 --> 00:02:24.717
Swapping simple-task demos for composite ones
recovers performance,

00:02:24.797 --> 00:02:28.890
isolating composition, not capability, as the bottleneck.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:30.658
The numbers tell it.

00:02:30.738 --> 00:02:35.651
Simple capitalization and swap reach about ninety
percent for Llama.

00:02:35.731 --> 00:02:40.129
The compose-by-step version falls to twenty percent
or lower,

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:42.038
with no gain from scaling.

00:02:42.118 --> 00:02:46.810
Milder separable cases climb to forty-four and
sixty-six percent.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:53.444
The takeaway: models combine two skills only when
they act on separate parts of the input.

00:02:53.524 --> 00:02:58.301
When a task needs genuinely chained, multi-step
reasoning, they fail,

00:02:58.381 --> 00:03:00.482
and more parameters won't help.

00:03:00.562 --> 00:03:03.720
Task structure predicts whether scaling helps.
